 * Guest kernel config:
   * CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK is on as of 2026-08-02 and pacman no longer needs
     --disable-sandbox. It cost two symbols -- SECURITY_LANDLOCK and the
     SECURITY_PATH it depends on -- and CONFIG_LSM already listed landlock
     first, so nothing else had to change. The guest says "landlock: Up and
     running." at boot and `pacman -Sy` returns 0 with the alpm sandbox active.
     Note that /sys/kernel/security does not exist in this guest, so the usual
     way of checking (cat /sys/kernel/security/lsm) reports nothing and looks
     like failure; securityfs is simply not mounted. The dmesg line is the
     check.
   * The guest .config is not in the tree and nothing reproduces it. Every
     option this port depends on -- KASAN off because the host's allocation
     lands in Linux's shadow region, RANDOMIZE_MEMORY off because the KASLR
     span encloses our window, DEBUG_ENTRY off because it ud2's on the real IF
     this design keeps set, and now SECURITY_LANDLOCK on -- is a decision made
     once and recorded nowhere a rebuild would find it.
   * "x86/mm: Checked W+X mappings: FAILED, 125045 W+X pages found" on every
     boot, from the kernel's own page-table auditor. The host builds the direct
     map with _PAGE_RW and no NX, so every page of it is writable and
     executable. It is harmless for a guest that runs at ring 0 anyway -- it has
     no privilege boundary to protect -- but nothing in the tree says so, and it
     reads like a security finding to whoever sees it next. Either mark the map
     NX where the guest never executes, or write down why it is not worth doing.

 * SOLVED 2026-08-04, kept so none of it is re-opened or re-derived. One
   session, six real bugs, and the order they were found in is misleading --
   each one hid the next.
   * The ltr race, which presented as "the guest randomly does not reach a
     shell". A selector load is a descriptor load, and ltr refuses a TSS
     descriptor whose busy bit is already set. The guest writes its TSS
     descriptor and is about to ltr when a hardware interrupt lands in the
     instructions between the two; the crossing re-enters through the switch,
     the switch's own ltr marks the descriptor busy, and the guest's ltr then
     takes #GP with error code 0x40 -- the TSS selector. Boot dies seconds in,
     and only when an interrupt hits a window a few instructions wide, which is
     why it looked like a coin flip weighted by how busy the host was. Fixed by
     clearing the busy bit again after ltr.
   * Which introduced the next one, and it is the most instructive thing here:
     doing that clear UNCONDITIONALLY left the HOST's TSS descriptor marked
     available instead of busy -- a standing modification to a live processor
     GDT. XP x64 is the first Windows with Kernel Patch Protection and a
     modified GDT is exactly what it checks: bugcheck 0x109
     CRITICAL_STRUCTURE_CORRUPTION, parameter 4 = 3 ("a processor GDT"),
     parameter 3 the host's own GDT base. PatchGuard checks on a randomized
     timer, so the machine died minutes later with the box idle and nothing of
     ours on the stack. That is why it read as spontaneous and unrelated, why
     traffic soaks could not reproduce it, and why the same evening produced
     0x1000007E and 0x3B bugchecks with STATUS_BREAKPOINT in unrelated drivers
     -- PatchGuard deliberately manifests as something else. Fixed by making the
     post-ltr clear apply only when the side being entered is the guest.
     The general rule this port already had, now paid for twice: the host's
     structures are not ours to edit. It is why antinx has no successor.
   * Blocking block I/O -- "defect 3", the one with a design note
     (doc/async-cobd-design). Every cobd transfer ran inline on the CPU-pinned
     monitor thread inside the crossing, so a single-processor guest made no
     progress of any kind for the duration of host disk I/O: measured 8.7 s of
     guest CPU over 5 m 14 s of wall clock, which tripped systemd's 3-minute
     service watchdogs and SIGABRT'd journald and userdbd. Now asynchronous:
     the monitor loop queues the request to kernel worker threads and returns
     to the guest at once, and completions come back through a ring in guest
     .bss (co_colinux_cobd_io) reaped on the cooperative tick. --sync-cobd
     keeps the old path for A/B.
   * Which introduced its own next bug: one global worker and one FIFO. The
     backing handle is FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO_NONALERT so requests to one unit
     serialize anyway, but with one queue requests to DIFFERENT units serialized
     too -- so a multi-minute write to the image being built blocked the root
     disk's reads behind it and the guest could not page in its own shell. The
     synchronous path had fairness by accident, one request at a time; a queue
     has to be given it deliberately. Now one worker and one queue per unit,
     each with its own wake event.
   * The multi-minute write itself: NTFS zero-fills from its valid-data length
     up to any write beyond it, synchronously inside that write. A freshly
     reserved 16 GB image has VDL zero and mke2fs writes backup superblocks near
     the end, so one request made the filesystem write gigabytes of zeroes.
     co_os_bdev_open now sets FileValidDataLengthInformation over the whole file,
     so it cannot happen whoever created the image.
   * intel_uncore_init killing PID 1. The guest's boot CPU is whichever
     processor the monitor thread was pinned to, so its APIC ID need not be the
     first the host firmware enumerates ("Boot CPU APIC ID not the first
     enumerated"), and intel_uncore then indexes its per-package array with
     garbage: an Oops in allocate_boxes and a dead init. A cooperative guest
     cannot use the uncore PMU anyway -- Windows owns the counters -- so the
     daemon adds initcall_blacklist=intel_uncore_init.
   * And the installer, which was failing in three ways at once. It polled the
     build log with `grep -a '==>\|RC='` and then tested the REPLY for "RC=" --
     a string its own command contains, so a console backlog that returned the
     echoed command read as a failed install ("The Linux install failed (grep
     ...)"). The marker is MOCO_RC= now and the tests are anchored at the start
     of the line. Its reply parser also fell back to returning the whole buffer
     when it could not find the echo boundary, which is what handed the command
     text to those tests; it returns nothing now and the caller polls again.
     Next was disabled for the entire second half of the wizard
     (`page < PG_INSTALL`), so a completed install could not advance and the
     final page had all three buttons dead at once. And the X server tickbox
     was cosmetic: `grep -i vcxsrv` over setup.c found exactly one hit and it
     was the label, so a user could tick it, watch the install succeed, click a
     Linux application and get nothing -- an X client with no server produces no
     error at all. It installs VcXsrv 1.14.2.1 now, ships it, and ships the
     launchers and shortcuts too.
   * Verified on the M92p end to end afterwards: the installer built a full
     Manjaro desktop onto a fresh 30 GB image over the network -- 217 console
     polls with ZERO timeouts, responses in 16-125 ms throughout, 1.3 GB
     downloaded, past both steps that had been "100% fatal", no bugcheck, and
     the guest responsive during the whole build rather than frozen for minutes.

 * STILL OPEN after that session, and worth stating precisely because it is
   easy to assume the above closed everything:
   * NOT the slirp bridge, and this correction is the point. It was filed here
     as "the bridge exits on its own during a heavy download", on the strength of
     one by-hand build that died when the bridge vanished mid-pacman. The
     timeline says otherwise: that run was on the driver from before the GDT fix,
     which went out at 01:21, and the bridge disappeared ahead of the 01:15
     bugcheck. On the fixed driver it stayed up through the whole 1.3 GB install.
     So the bridge was doing exactly what it is written to do -- 100 consecutive
     ring-read failures at a 20 ms poll means the guest is gone, so leave, so the
     driver can be unloaded and the binaries replaced. The guest really was gone;
     it was dying of the GDT corruption. A symptom got filed as a defect, which
     is the same mistake as reading a quiet sample as a dead system, and it was
     made three times in one evening.
   * One bugcheck of that evening is still unexplained: 0x3B in afd.sys,
     STATUS_BREAKPOINT at ntoskrnl+0x26e20, zero linux.sys frames. It predates
     the GDT modification, so it cannot share that cause. It has not recurred,
     and there is nothing to go on beyond one dump -- so it is recorded rather
     than theorised about. If it comes back, the thing to establish first is
     whether a driver/daemon swap or a taskkill happened near it, since both have
     produced handle-teardown crashes here before.
   * colinux-daemon.exe --run cannot be deployed while a guest is running: the
     running daemon holds its own image, so the desktop shortcuts keep using
     whatever binary was there when the guest started. Harmless, but it means a
     driver/daemon swap needs the guest stopped first, and nothing says so.
   * The guest-side async cobd changes are not in the snapshot diff yet. They
     live in patch/7.1.5/async-cobd-src/ as whole files (cobd.c, process.c),
     deliberately, until the design has had more than one successful run behind
     it. patch/7.1.5/current-tree-snapshot.diff must be regenerated from the
     pristine tarball before anyone else can build this kernel.
   * Async cobd is one worker per unit, which is the right shape but not the
     limit: a single unit still serializes on its FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO_NONALERT
     handle. Parallelising one disk needs the handle reopened without that flag
     and an event per request. doc/async-cobd-design section 3.8 has the
     reasoning; nothing needs it yet.

 * The root filesystem is reproducible now, but only in one direction:
   * DONE: tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh builds a bootable Manjaro image from
     nothing -- skeleton, packages, keyring (pacman-key --init && --populate,
     without which every package fails verification as TRUST_UNDEFINED while
     its signature is perfectly good), mirrors, ldconfig, fstab, autologin,
     10-eth0.network with systemd-networkd, IPv6 off, DISPLAY, and a user.
     It takes an image file or a block device.
   * DONE, 2026-08-03: it has now run on a build host -- CachyOS, as root,
     building the seed. It needed two fixes to work there, and both had been
     failing inside the guest too, unnoticed:
       - the chroot had no /etc/resolv.conf, so every name lookup in it failed
         and pacman reported "Could not resolve host" for every mirror in the
         list. That is what the mirror-ranking comment blamed on dead servers.
         The cost was the whole re-verification step, which had therefore
         never run in any build this project has ever made.
       - the final `du -sm` walked the bound /proc, so the reported image size
         included the host's and the run ended in a page of errors about pids
         that vanished mid-walk. It asks df now.
   * The re-verification proves less than the comment claimed, and now says
     so. `pacman -Syu` checks the signed databases and that every installed
     name and version matches them, which catches a tampered or stale mirror.
     It does not check the signature of each package file already on disk:
     those went down under SigLevel = Never and, with nothing to upgrade,
     pacman never looks at them again. Closing it means either installing the
     keyring first and everything else with signatures enforced -- which needs
     a working chroot before the keyring exists, so it is not a one-liner --
     or fetching the .sig beside each cached package and verifying by hand
     before the cache is deleted.
   * The image also has to get smaller before it can ship. 6 GB with about a
     gigabyte used is mostly zeroes, so zstd takes it to a few hundred
     megabytes; something then has to expand it on first run.
   * And a rule that cost an evening: changes made inside a running guest are
     only on disk after a clean poweroff. stop.bat and the run deadline do not
     unmount, so a file written seconds earlier comes back as a journaled inode
     with no data -- zero bytes, which looks like the write never happened.
 * The seed image carries its own copy of the maker script, and it goes stale:
   * The installer runs /root/mk.sh out of root-arch.img, preferring it over
     /usr/local/bin/mkmanjarorootfs.sh. On 2026-08-04 that copy was the
     pre-geoip version: the tree had been switched to `pacman-mirrors --geoip`
     precisely to stop the build timing all ~125 mirrors, and the install still
     did the slow ranking, because the tree is not what runs -- the image is.
     Nothing reported a mismatch, and nothing could: the file is inside an ext4
     image that no build step ever looks into.
   * The copy in dist-x64/root-arch.img has been refreshed by hand (debugfs
     write + e2fsck, verified byte-identical to the tree script). That fixes
     the next install and nothing after it. release/MoCoLinux-0.1.0 still ships
     the old one, deliberately -- its sha256.txt describes what was published.
   * The real fix is for Setup to put the current script into the guest before
     invoking it, rather than trusting whatever the image happens to carry. It
     already has a console conversation and the script is 40 KB; the same
     argument applies to any other tool the build depends on. Until then, every
     change to tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh has to be written into the seed image
     by hand or it does not exist as far as an install is concerned.

 * The box-side scripts are not in the tree:
   * deploy.sh, run-arch.bat, run-linux.bat, console.bat, net.bat, stop.bat,
     reboot.bat, dbg-start.bat, verifier-on/off.bat and getdumps.bat all live in
     dist-x64/, which is outside the repository. Every one of them encodes
     something learned the hard way -- why the debug stream must be launched
     detached, why shutdown.exe has to be called by full path, why a deploy
     verifies by reading files back -- and none of it is version controlled.
     tools/harden-m92p.bat is in the tree; the rest should be too.

 * KREAD has no lock against teardown:
   * CO_MANAGER_IOCTL_KREAD walks the guest's page tables with no protection
     against co_kload_free destroying the address space underneath it. It is
     safe today only because its one caller is the boot daemon itself,
     sequenced before its own KLOAD_END. Any second process using it would be
     the use-after-free that the CONET path needed net_lock to avoid. Either
     give it the same retire-under-lock treatment or document that it is
     single-caller by construction.

 * i386 does not build:
   * src/colinux/kernel/kload.c uses CO_ARCH_PGD_INDEX and _PAGE_GLOBAL, which
     exist only in arch/x86_64/mmu.h, so the i386 target has not compiled since
     f2ed1128. Nothing in this port needs i386, but "the 32-bit build still
     works" was a stated property of the early milestones and it quietly stopped
     being true. Either exclude the x86-64-only kernel loader from that build or
     drop the claim.

 * Consoles (this port's, not the 2004 ones):
   * colinux-daemon --console serves one client at a time: listen(backlog 1) and
     a single accept loop, so a second connection is reset rather than refused
     with a reason, and there is no way to look at a guest somebody else is
     already attached to.
   * colinux-console-nt cannot attach to an x86-64 guest at all. It opens a
     monitor instance (co_user_monitor_open with CO_MODULE_CONSOLE) and expects
     screen-buffer updates from the guest's coconsole driver; this port creates
     no monitor instance and the guest uses hvc, which is a byte stream. Making
     it work means either porting the monitor path and coconsole, or teaching it
     to read the CONSOLE ioctl and parse VT sequences itself.

 * moco-boot.vbs cannot tell the boot daemon from the console server:
   * It decides whether a guest is already running with
     Running("colinux-daemon.exe") -- but the console server is that same
     executable (`colinux-daemon.exe --console 2323`). So an orphaned console
     server left over from a previous run makes the launcher believe the guest
     is up: it starts the X server and quits, and no guest is booted, silently.
   * Found while writing R3's soak, where every cycle after the first tested
     nothing at all and looked like it had passed. The soak drives the boot
     daemon directly now, which is the right thing for a test, but the launcher
     still has the bug for real use.
   * The fix is to match on the command line rather than the image name, or to
     have the boot daemon publish something the launcher can ask about -- it
     already has a single-instance mutex, so asking whether that is held is
     both cheaper and exact.

 * Guest RAM is freed and re-allocated every run, and that fragments the host:
   * QUANTIFIED 2026-08-04 while soaking R3, and the number is better than the
     entry below implies: on Windows 7, four consecutive boot/teardown cycles
     each got the full 1024 MB back. What fails first is not the gigabyte, it
     is block 0 -- the ~44 MB unbroken run for the image plus page tables --
     with CO_RC(OUT_OF_MEMORY) at kload.c:702. The allocator visibly works
     harder on the way there, needing 64, 66, 65 then 89 blocks for the same
     gigabyte as the free pool breaks up.
   * Driver Verifier's special pool makes it far worse and was on for that run,
     so four cycles is a floor rather than the number to quote.
   * A driver reload does NOT cure it, because it does not defragment host
     physical memory; a host reboot does, immediately and completely. That
     asymmetry is the quickest way to tell this apart from a leak in our own
     accounting, which a reload would clear.
   * The symptom, which is worth stating first because it looks like a driver
     bug and is not: the first boot after `sc start` is reliable, and a second
     boot on the same loaded driver makes the whole box sluggish or wedges it
     outright, from the instant the daemon starts and before the guest executes
     an instruction. A reboot cures it every time and nothing else does.
   * The cause is that co_kload_begin opens with co_kload_free, so every run
     hands its guest RAM back to Windows and the next one asks for all of it
     again. At 2 GB in 32 MB pieces that is around sixty-four
     MmAllocateContiguousMemory calls per boot, each a search of the PFN
     database for an unbroken physical run, with the memory manager's locks held
     and working-set trimming when it cannot find one.
     On a freshly booted host physical memory is one long stretch and those
     searches are cheap. After a run has cycled a gigabyte or two through the
     contiguous allocator the address space is holed, and the same sixty-four
     searches have to hunt for runs that no longer exist in one piece. So the
     state that is not reset between runs is not ours -- it is Windows'
     physical memory, and we are the ones fragmenting it.
   * The targeted fix is to stop giving it back. Allocate the guest's RAM once
     per driver load and reuse it across runs: nothing else can use those blocks
     while the driver is loaded, one guest runs at a time by construction, and
     the second and tenth boots then cost zero contiguous searches. co_kload_free
     keeps the blocks and destroys only the address space; a new
     co_kload_release_memory drops them from the manager's unload path, where
     co_kload_free is already called.
   * Three things that have to be got right, and the reason this was not done
     in passing. Block 0 holds the image and the page tables, so a reused block
     needs kload_table_top reset and a freshly built space rather than the old
     one. The previous run's RAM contents survive into the next boot, which is
     harmless only because .bss arrives as explicit zeroing chunks from the
     loader -- if that ever stops being true, a guest inherits the last one's
     memory. And this is the teardown path, which has produced a bugcheck 0xC2
     (double free, fixed with kload_lock) and a host freeze (per-entry
     MmUnmapIoSpace in co_space_free_table) in a single evening; it deserves its
     own change and its own five-point audit rather than a rider on something
     else.
   * The real fix underneath is pseudo-physical memory. The contiguity
     requirement exists only because guest physical must equal host physical,
     which is forced by Linux calling __va() on entries it reads out of its own
     page tables. A p2m/machine-frame layer -- which coLinux-i386 and Xen both
     have -- removes it entirely, and with it the whole class of problem: RAM
     becomes ordinary scattered pages, there is no e820 entry limit to work
     around, and CO_KLOAD_MAX_BLOCKS stops existing. That is a milestone, not a
     fix.

 * One-click bring-up:
   * Most of the prerequisites landed while chasing other things, so what is
     left is the launcher itself rather than the reasons it could not exist.
   * DONE, and each was a blocker: the guest configures its own network at boot
     (10-eth0.network + systemd-networkd, written by tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh,
     so it is in the tree rather than in one image); CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK
     and a real mirrorlist, without which pacman refuses to fetch anything; the
     console and the slirp bridge exit on their own when the guest ends, so a
     launcher can wait for its children instead of being unable to stop them;
     every daemon refuses to start twice, so a retry or a second launch cannot
     produce two bridges competing for one guest's rings; and xstart.bat
     returns instead of wedging its caller, which `start` cannot do because it
     hands its console handles to the child whatever you do -- WScript.Shell's
     Run does not.
   * DONE 2026-08-04, and not in the shape this entry expected. The launcher is
     moco-boot.vbs rather than a mode of the daemon: it installs the driver if
     needed, starts it, does nothing if a guest is already running, and starts
     the guest, the slirp bridge, the console server and the X server -- all
     hidden, because those four are services with nothing for a person to read
     and a minimised window is still a taskbar button in the way. A .vbs because
     no cmd construct can start a windowless detached child: `start /min`
     flashes a console and `start /b` hands its own handles to the child and
     wedges, which this project has already lost an evening to.
   * And there IS a colinux-daemon.exe --run now, for a different job: it starts
     one application inside the running guest and returns, by writing a line
     into the guest's console ring through the driver. That is what the desktop
     shortcuts use. It went through the driver rather than the console server's
     TCP port for two reasons -- it does not compete for that server's single
     client slot, so an open terminal keeps working, and more importantly it
     needs no Python. The first version of the launchers was a Python script
     opening a socket, which works only on a machine that happens to have
     Python; a stock XP has none, so every icon would have silently done
     nothing, which is the hardest failure in this system to diagnose.
   * moco-icons.vbs makes the desktop shortcuts (Konsole, xterm, Kate, Firefox,
     Dolphin, System Monitor, a terminal, and Start MoCoLinux) and the All Users
     Startup entry, so Manjaro comes up at logon. The terminal shortcut is
     telnet.exe, not a Python client: XP ships telnet, and the console server
     negotiates SUPPRESS-GO-AHEAD and ECHO on connect precisely so a default
     telnet client behaves like a raw socket.
   * The installer ships and runs all of it: the launchers are in `payload`, and
     install_shortcuts() hands both directories to moco-icons.vbs. Neither that
     nor the X server install is fatal -- missing icons are an inconvenience, a
     failed install is not.
 * SOLVED, kept only so it is not re-opened: the 32-bit triple fault.
   * A selector load is a descriptor load. The world switch restored the guest's
     saved FS/GS by `mov %ax,%gs`, and a 32-bit task runs with GS = 0x63, which
     is TLS entry 12 -- an entry Linux rewrites on every context switch.
     __switch_to zeroes entries 12-14 (process_64.c:632) some distance before it
     replaces the outgoing selector (:663), a window it is entitled to because
     it runs under local_irq_disable() -- which in this guest is virtual only.
     A host interrupt landing there produced a re-entry that loaded an all-zero
     descriptor: #GP with no IST, frame pushed on the host's stack which is
     unmapped in the guest CR3, #PF, #DF, whose IST needs the host's TSS through
     a TR not yet loaded. Triple fault, no dump, nothing logged.
   * Fixed by validating the descriptor before loading FS or GS and substituting
     null otherwise; the FS_BASE/GS_BASE restore carries the state. See the
     commit for the full reasoning.
   * The reproducer, if anything in this area is ever touched again:
         for i in $(seq 5000); do /usr/bin/gio-querymodules-32 /tmp >/dev/null 2>&1; done
     with a desktop workload live. Thirty seconds to a reset before the fix,
     nine seconds and healthy after. 12,000 of the same on an idle guest never
     failed -- the second runnable process is what makes the window frequent
     enough to hit, so any test without one proves nothing.
 * The guest has no swap, and it shows as 97% system time:
   * Measured with Steam running: load average 6 on one vCPU, 877 MB of 929 used,
     13 MB free, 52 MB available, no swap at all -- and %Cpu(s) 2.7 us, 97.3 sy.
     Ninety-seven per cent of the guest's CPU is kernel time and three per cent
     is the application.
   * With no swap the kernel cannot page anything out, so it sits in permanent
     direct reclaim, scanning and evicting on nearly every allocation. That is
     where the system time goes. It is not rendering and it is not X latency,
     which is what everyone assumes when a remote desktop feels slow.
   * tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh should create a swapfile and an fstab entry for it.
     The disk has room and it converts relentless reclaim into occasional
     paging, which is the difference between unusable and merely slow.
   * The other half is real and not fixable this way: load 6 on one processor.
     Four Steam processes plus the kernel's own work, six-times oversubscribed.
     That is the SMP entry, not this one.
   * Worth trying --mem 1536 alongside. 2048 failed on contiguous allocation, but
     that was on a host that had already run a guest; on a fresh boot it may be
     available.

 * co_debug_read has a use-after-free:
   * bugcheck 0xD5 DRIVER_PAGE_FAULT_IN_FREED_SPECIAL_POOL, p3 resolving to
     linux.sys+0x2334 = co_debug_read+0x44. Caught only because Driver Verifier's
     special pool is enabled; without it this is a silent read of freed memory.
   * It fired during startup, not under any guest load, so it is unrelated to
     anything the guest does. What makes it worth fixing promptly is what it is:
     the debug daemon's own drain path. That daemon is the one instrument whose
     records survive a host reset, which is how the guest-RAM allocation freeze
     and the 32-bit triple fault were both localised -- an instrument that can
     itself bugcheck the machine is worse than no instrument, because it makes
     every result it produces suspect.
   * Likely the same family as the disabled resize_section (see the 0x4E entry):
     a section freed while a reader still holds a pointer into it.

 * An unexplained #GP in trap_init, seen once:
   * A Manjaro boot stopped with vector 13 at co_colinux_adopt_traps+0x150,
     error code 0x40. The error code is a selector, not an address: index 8 in
     the GDT, which on x86-64 is GDT_ENTRY_TSS, so the processor refused the ltr
     that loads the guest's task register. The kernel log ends at the
     inode-cache line, which is vfs_caches_init_early() -- the call immediately
     before trap_init() -- so the stopping point and the faulting address agree.
   * It has not recurred, on the same vmlinux, across many boots since. That is
     not the same as fixed, and it is recorded here rather than forgotten
     because an intermittent fault in the one function that installs the guest's
     descriptor tables is the kind of thing that comes back during a
     demonstration. Candidates worth reading when it does: whether the TSS
     descriptor's busy bit can be set on entry (the switch clears it before
     every ltr in both directions, but adopt_traps does its own lgdt/ltr/lidt
     outside that), and whether the descriptor's base can be built from a
     per-CPU TSS address before the guest has settled which CPU it is on.

 * The release artifacts are not built by anything:
   * Mostly answered, and by a different route than this entry assumed. The
     shipped download does not contain a built Manjaro at all: it contains the
     driver, the daemons, vmlinux, the ~1.2 GB Arch seed image that carries the
     builder, VcXsrv 1.14.2.1, the launchers and Setup. The target then builds
     its own Manjaro over the network with tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh, which is
     what the successful end-to-end run did. So there is no multi-gigabyte
     desktop image to produce, compress or expand on first run -- that whole
     problem went away with the decision in doc/installer.
   * DONE: mkmanjarorootfs.sh has now run on a build host as well as inside a
     guest, which is what closed "reproducible by someone else". Two silent
     failures came out of that: no /etc/resolv.conf in the chroot (so every
     mirror "could not resolve" and the trust-on-first-use re-verification never
     ran, while the script still said "done, unmounted clean"), and a `du -sm`
     that walked the host's /proc because it ran before the unmount.
   * What is left is packaging discipline rather than capability: nothing yet
     assembles those files into one directory and checks that the set is
     complete, so the payload list in setup.c is the only description of what a
     release must contain, and a missing entry is discovered by a user rather
     than by a build. The seed image is also still produced by hand.
   * The mirror ranking is no longer part of the cost: --fasttrack timed all
     ~125 servers including the dead ones, which was minutes of the build doing
     nothing visible. It is --geoip now, with --fasttrack only as the fallback.

 * Real OpenGL in the guest, which nothing here can currently deliver:
   * Where it stands. The guest has no GPU, no DRI device and pci=off, so its
     Mesa falls back to llvmpipe and renders in its single core -- see
     "GPU: Mesa llvmpipe" in the README screenshot. LIBGL_ALWAYS_INDIRECT=1
     with `-wgl +iglx` on the X server moves the work to the host's real card
     instead, and that is worth having, but it is capped at roughly OpenGL 1.4:
     indirect GLX is a wire protocol and nobody ever defined encodings for 3.x
     or 4.x core profiles. The card's own capability is irrelevant to that
     ceiling -- a GT730 doing 4.6 for Windows applications still cannot be
     reached past 1.4 by a client speaking GLX.
   * So there are exactly two things a guest application can have today: a
     complete-but-slow software renderer, or hardware acceleration limited to
     an OpenGL from 2002. Anything wanting a modern core profile -- Chromium
     and therefore Steam's client, most compositors, most games -- refuses
     rather than degrades.
   * What would actually fix it. Note first what the constraint is NOT: the
     card and its driver are not the limit, and neither is translation. The
     340.x driver on XP gives this GT730 OpenGL 4.4, and wined3d maps D3D onto
     that well enough to run GTA V. A translation layer on this hardware is a
     solved problem.
     The limit is the address-space boundary. wined3d translates inside the
     application's own process -- a call is a call, nothing is marshalled --
     while anything here has to cross. Per-call round trips are fatal at any
     frequency however good the renderer at the far end, and that, rather than
     rendering quality, is what makes virgl-style designs disappointing.
     So the design question is not which renderer but where to cut, and the
     answer is: as coarsely as possible.
     - Serialise at a submit boundary, not a call boundary. Ship whole command
       buffers and let the host replay them against real OpenGL. The transport
       is the least of it -- these rings already carry 140 MB/s on the block
       path and a crossing costs microseconds (the 16 MB/s network figure is
       TCP through NAT, not the ring's ceiling). The work is the guest-side
       Mesa backend, and it is a project rather than a patch.

       Worth being explicit about why this is more tractable here than the
       equivalent in a virtual machine, because it is the argument for
       attempting it at all: guest physical IS host physical. There is no
       second address space to marshal resources into. A vertex buffer or a
       texture the guest application writes sits in memory the host can already
       address by arithmetic (co_kload_frame_va on the page), so the replay
       reads those buffers in place, where the application put them. Zero copy
       is not an optimisation to add later, it is what happens if nothing is
       done to prevent it -- and eliminating that copy is most of what virtio
       designs spend their complexity on.

       Presentation lands the same way. An X client here already owns a real
       Windows window, created by the server in multiwindow mode. If the
       host-side GL context is a native WGL context on that HWND, the finished
       frame never moves: no readback, no image shipped anywhere, and
       SwapBuffers is the ordinary one. That is the step VirtualGL cannot skip
       and the reason its ceiling is lower than this design's.

       What would still separate it from native, so nobody is surprised later:
       synchronisation points -- glFinish, glReadPixels, fences, occlusion
       queries -- are real round trips and no transport speed removes them; and
       the guest has one processor, so for anything substantial the application
       is CPU-bound in the guest long before GL is the limit. That second one
       is the SMP entry, not this one.
     - VirtualGL's arrangement is the same idea with the split at libGL and
       images coming back instead of pixels going forward. It normally assumes
       the rendering host owns a real GPU and an X server, which is an
       assumption that is usually false and happens to be exactly true here,
       so it may reach a working state far sooner for the same result.
     - Actual passthrough of the card to the guest. Not credible: there is no
       IOMMU in play, the guest deliberately touches no host hardware, and
       Windows is still driving the GT730 while the guest runs.
   * MEASURED 2026-08-04, and the answer is that 1.4-with-acceleration does not
     cover it. `glhw glxinfo -B` in the guest, on Windows 7 with the GT 730:
         direct rendering: No (LIBGL_ALWAYS_INDIRECT set)
         OpenGL vendor:   NVIDIA Corporation
         OpenGL renderer: GeForce GT 730/PCIe/SSE2
         OpenGL version:  1.4 (4.6.0 NVIDIA 388.13)
     The card is reached and its driver is 4.6-capable; the GLX wire protocol
     is what caps it, exactly as argued above. It is worse than a cap, though:
     the same run died with GLXBadContext on X_GLXMakeCurrent, and the default
     (software) path does not get that far -- glxinfo fails with GLXBadDrawable
     on X_GLXGetDrawableAttributes, a 2026 Mesa talking to a 2013 server. So
     neither path currently produces a usable context for a client that asks
     for one properly.
   * And the cost is not confined to games. KDE's System Settings comes up as a
     black window: Plasma 6's configuration UI is QML, Qt Quick wants a GL
     context, and there is not one to be had. QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software renders
     it perfectly, which is the proof that the application is fine and the
     context is what is missing. The applications that have always worked --
     Konsole, Kate, Dolphin -- are QtWidgets drawn by QPainter on the CPU, so
     they never asked. Expect the same black window from plasma-systemmonitor,
     Discover and most modern settings modules. That moves this entry from a
     performance question to a usability one: today roughly half of Plasma's
     own interface does not draw.
   * And note the two settings are useless apart. LIBGL_ALWAYS_INDIRECT without
     `+iglx` gets refused by the server; `-wgl` without LIBGL_ALWAYS_INDIRECT
     accelerates nothing, because the guest has already finished drawing before
     the server sees a pixel. Both are set now, and neither has been tested.

 * The guest kernel configuration is not in the tree:
   * Nothing records which options this port needs, so a vmlinux built from
     patch/7.1.5/current-tree-snapshot.diff and x86_64_defconfig is missing
     things whose absence is invisible until userspace fails oddly:
     CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK (pacman 7 sandboxes its downloads and refuses to
     fetch anything without it), CONFIG_USER_NS (Steam, bwrap, flatpak and
     Chromium all want unprivileged user namespaces), and the negatives that are
     load-bearing -- KASAN off because the host's allocation lands in PML4 slot
     501 inside Linux's KASAN shadow, RANDOMIZE_MEMORY off because the KASLR
     span encloses our window, DEBUG_ENTRY off because it ud2's on the real IF
     at the exception return paths, which is the bit this design keeps set.
   * A config fragment plus the scripts/config invocations that apply it, so the
     kernel is reproducible from the tree rather than from whatever is in
     build/linux-7.1.5-coop/.config on one machine.

 * Debug daemon, after networking and pacman -S work:
   * It needs a clean shutdown. `colinux-debug-daemon -d` holds a handle on
     \\.\CoLinuxDriver forever and blocks inside a co_debug_read ioctl waiting
     for records, so there is no way to ask it to stop. `sc stop
     CoLinuxDriver` then reports success and parks the service in
     STOP_PENDING: the I/O manager will not call DriverUnload while a handle
     is open, and the driver stays resident and half-stopped.

     That leaves only two ways to swap a driver, and both are worse than the
     problem. taskkill ends the process but cannot interrupt its thread while
     that thread is inside the driver, so the handles close afterwards and the
     driver is released underneath kernel-mode code -- which is precisely the
     bugcheck 0x50 in the handle-close path that took this box down four times
     in one evening. Or reboot, which is what we do, at a couple of minutes
     each and several times a session.

     What it wants is a way to be asked: an event the daemon waits on
     alongside the ring, or a second ioctl that makes the blocking read
     return, so it can close its handle and exit on request. Then
     reload-driver.sh stops needing to kill anything, and `sc stop` means what
     it says.

 * A 0x50 in ExFreePool at end-of-run, not root-caused:
   * On 2026-08-02 a teardown bugchecked 0x50 with the faulting instruction at
     ntoskrnl+0xaf8a3, reached through dispatch_wrapper -> co_manager_ioctl ->
     co_arch_boot_loaded -> co_arch_extable_free. That is ExFreePool reading
     pool metadata for the 18 KB exception table, so the block's header was
     already corrupt when it was freed -- something wrote into pool near it.
     co_arch_extable_free nulls after freeing, so a double free of that pointer
     is not the mechanism, and the table itself is unchanged at 1516 entries in
     every run before and after.
   * It has not reproduced since, and the previous 0x50 series in this port was
     explicitly intermittent. Special pool does not cover it: Driver Verifier
     only special-pools allocations of a page or less, and this is 18 KB, so an
     overrun into it is invisible until the header is read at free time. Some
     way of catching the write rather than the free is what this needs.
   * logs/repro.sh drives boot / pacman -Sy / poweroff cycles looking for it. It
     must never kill a process to clean up between cycles -- see the note at the
     top of it, which cost a box.

 * co_arch_guest_space_destroy walks tables it does not own:
   * It reports "guest space: freed N tables, owned M" whenever the two
     disagree, and they have disagreed on every teardown in the history of this
     port: 33/32, 35/32, 42/32, 46/34, and 62/64 and 63/65 where it freed fewer
     than it owns. Nobody read the line.
   * freed > owned because the walk descends into subtrees the space did not
     create -- the kernel's own level3_kernel_pgt, and the three host grafts at
     PML4 slots 273, 289 and 501. freed < owned because tables the space did
     create are no longer reachable from the top by the time it walks, the
     guest having replaced entries with its own. Neither is fatal today: with a
     frame source set nothing is actually put back, and the pages all live
     inside the blocks that kload_release_pages frees wholesale.
   * The fix is to record which top-level entries were grafted and not descend
     into them, which also stops the walk touching host page tables with
     MmMapIoSpace at all.

 * colinux-daemon.exe --install-driver does not start the driver:
   * It reports "driver already installed /
     daemon: driver installed" and exits 0 when the service exists but is
     STOPPED, which is its state after every reboot. It does not start it.
     Anything that treats that success as "the driver is loaded" then fails
     with "manager open: The system cannot find the file specified" on every
     subsequent run. It should start the service, or say that it did not.

 * SMP -- the milestone the swap and OpenGL entries defer to. Staged plan
   agreed 2026-08-07; rungs are S0..S6, each hardware-verified and committed
   before the next starts.
   * S0 DONE 2026-08-07 (commit 26638a3d), verified on the M92p under Windows
     10: --test-smp runs two threads pinned to two processors, each crossing
     into a guest of its own, concurrently. 10M crossings per lane in 54 s
     (~2.7 us each), counters and rbx exact both lanes, 5671 host interrupts
     replayed from inside the guests on both cores at once, lane-distinct MSR
     sentinels (FS_BASE/GS_BASE/KERNEL_GS_BASE/LSTAR) intact throughout, zero
     host-verify escalations, zero migrations. KSTOP mid-run ends both lanes
     cleanly; the boot regression after it is clean. The central claim --
     per-crossing state is fully per-passage-page and two cores can switch
     worlds independently -- is now measured, not argued.
   * The design in one paragraph: each vCPU is a daemon thread in a blocking
     per-vCPU ioctl, pinned to its own core, with a passage block of its own
     (incl. GDT/TSS/IDT/stubs/FXSAVE). Guest-side machine globals
     (co_colinux_virtual_if, co_colinux_passage_page) become fixed arrays
     indexed by a raw GS read of cpu_number -- host-visible, so never percpu.
     APIC cannot be config'd out on x86-64: an inert struct apic (Xen PV's
     xen_pv_apic shape), fake topology, and an smp_prepare_cpus override
     replace nolapic. AP bringup is the BSP's kick_ap crossing handing the
     host an explicit initial-state block (the KBOOT shape, once per AP).
     IPIs are shared-memory pending bitmaps + an idle doorbell + opportunistic
     ring-3 frame surgery; reschedule is arrival-only. TLB shootdown needs no
     IPI at all: every crossing already clears CR4.PGE around the CR3 write
     (full flush, globals included, nopcid), so a sequence counter + in-host
     flag per vCPU answers "has it flushed" -- kick and wait for the counter.
   * S1 DONE 2026-08-07 (commits d268bbaf host, 0dd6c60c guest), verified on
     the M92p under Windows 10. Host: the monitor loop's statics became
     co_vcpu_t fields (passage page, replayed-vector table, run flag); the
     abort flag became a broadcast and co_arch_boot_running() now means "any
     vCPU still inside"; the idle doorbell went from one auto-reset event
     (which releases exactly ONE waiter -- a lost wake with two vCPUs, a
     correctness bug not a fairness one) to one per vCPU plus a wake-all for
     device doorbells. A --test-smp lane is now a vCPU in the slot of its own
     number, so KSTOP and unload have one mechanism instead of two. Loop
     locals deliberately stayed locals: a second vCPU is a second thread with
     its own stack. Guest: co_colinux_virtual_if and co_colinux_passage_page
     became arrays indexed by co_colinux_vcpu_id(), which is 0 under SMP=n
     and a deliberate #error under SMP=y.
     **Parity was measured, not assumed**: the rebuilt vmlinux disassembles
     to 7,756,149 instructions identical to the previous build, differing
     only in the version string and one renumbered constant-pool label. Plus
     5M concurrent crossings per lane, bounded boot regression matching the
     baseline (vgpu timeout 128.62s vs 128.43s), and the full Manjaro guest
     booting on the new driver and kernel -- coterm reports #74, load 0.01.
     The guest-side snapshot was regenerated against the released tarball and
     reverse-apply checked; it now covers 34 files, including the two the
     README recorded as missing.
   * Remaining rungs: S2 SMP=y kernel with one CPU (fake apic, topology, drop
     nolapic, and implement co_colinux_vcpu_id() for real; gate:
     explain the one-off #GP in co_colinux_adopt_traps first); S3 second vCPU
     to idle (KVCPU_RUN + START_VCPU/KICK_VCPU ops, cpu_bringup per Xen
     smp_pv.c); S4 IPIs + per-CPU timer + shootdown under TLB-torture and
     make -j2; S5 device-ring locking + desktop/Steam soak; S6 --cpus 3 and
     oneshot/NO_HZ evaluation. Host core budget: 4 cores = Windows + cogpu
     spin-poll + 2 vCPUs, zero slack -- the daemon should print the core map.

 * Cleanups:
   * Merge console into console-base.
   * Clear out some function naming brain damage and make more use
     of the 'static' keyword :).
   * The usual tidbits: comments, refactoring, etc.
